interface Person {
  name: string;
}

interface Person {
  name: number;
}
```
This code throws an error because the properties are incompatible.

**Intersections:** With intersection types, properties with different types are merged automatically. When the type is used later, TypeScript expects the property to satisfy both types simultaneously, which may produce unexpected results.

```ts
interface Person1 {
  name: string;
}

interface Person2 {
  name: number;
}

type Staff = Person1 & Person2
```
This code compiles, but results in a `never` type. Here, `Staff` would require the `name` property to be both a string and a number, so the property is of type `never`.

**Related note (TypeScript 3.9+):** When the conflicting properties are discriminant properties (such as differing literal `kind` fields), the type system collapses the whole intersection type to `never` instead of just the property.

