`Omit<Type, Keys>` constructs a type by picking all properties from `Type` and then removing `Keys` (a string literal or union of string literals). It is the opposite of `Pick`. It was introduced in TypeScript 3.5.

Example from the docs:

```ts
type Person = {
  name: string;
  age: number;
  location: string;
};

type QuantumPerson = Omit<Person, "location">;

// equivalent to
type QuantumPerson = {
  name: string;
  age: number;
};
```

This copies over all the properties of `Person` except for `location`. You can also omit multiple keys with a union, such as `Omit<Todo, "completed" | "createdAt">`.

Earlier release notes say that `Omit<T, K>` can be written as `Pick<T, Exclude<keyof T, K>>`.

