This works because of TypeScript's function parameter bivariance. When comparing function parameter types, assignment succeeds if either the source parameter is assignable to the target parameter, or vice versa. Since `MyMouseEvent` extends `Event`, the two types are related in one direction, so the callback is accepted.

This is intentionally unsound: a caller might end up being given a function that takes a more specialized type, but invokes the function with a less specialized type. In practice, this sort of error is rare, and allowing it enables many common JavaScript patterns.

Example from the docs:

```ts
interface Event { timestamp: number; }
interface MyMouseEvent extends Event { x: number; y: number; }

function listenEvent(eventType: EventType, handler: (n: Event) => void) { /* ... */ }

// Unsound, but useful and common
listenEvent(EventType.Mouse, (e: MyMouseEvent) => console.log(e.x + "," + e.y));
```

Wholly incompatible types are still rejected, e.g. `(e: number) => console.log(e)`.

If you want TypeScript to flag this, enable the compiler flag `strictFunctionTypes`.

