No, not explicitly. With `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` enabled, an optional property `x?: number` means "either absent or a `number`", not "`number | undefined`". So this errors:

```ts
interface Options {
  size?: number;
}

const a: Options = {};              // OK
const b: Options = { size: 10 };    // OK
const c: Options = { size: undefined }; // Error
```

If you want to allow `undefined` explicitly, add it to the type:

```ts
interface Options {
  size?: number | undefined;
}
```

Reading the property still gives `number | undefined` (since it might be missing), but writing `undefined` is only allowed if the type includes it. The flag is not part of `strict`, so you must enable it separately.

